  {
    "path": "frontier/sources/i138_nonlinear_dynamics_discovery/decoded/iteration123-thermal-production-stability-task-brief.txt",
    "sha256": "1b68db43d7e22a9c2767b0c989ca3c66b72184b09e0c69db7127605588b430f4",
    "field": null,
    "start": 43,
    "end": 85,
    "total": 603,
    "range_sha256": "7631fda46d266091501c9dc4e5ee0e796161ecade1a495f62d4e12be4998fbdb",
    "raw_lf": 603,
    "terminal_lf": true,
    "ending": "For the edge `(u,v)=(v_k-e_1,v_k)`, the logarithmic-mean identity gives exactly T4, with its numerator sign matched to the logarithm. The numerator before multiplication by k is `(r+1)c/k+O(exp(-a_0 k))`; the logarithm is `k h(x_k)+O(log k)`. Thus the displayed contribution divided by k tends to `(r+1)c`. The off-diagonal test is centered. All other nonexceptional edges form a geometric first-moment sum, so Q is finite at each k but diverges along the sequence. This is a counterexample to every vanishing weighted-Q or input-relative-entropy transfer on the original constraint set. It says nothing against trace stability, and is not a proof about ordinary R.",
    "display_status": "supplied"
  },
